description:
## Verified finding

The repository has no bench target, Criterion configuration, performance fixture, or machine-readable timing output. The ignored Docker integration test is intentionally a lifecycle regression test containing fixed waits, lock contention, failure injection, and polling, so its total runtime cannot serve as a throughput baseline.

The audit produced useful but ad hoc measurements. Structural performance changes need a repeatable baseline before implementation.

## Target

Add a checked-in benchmark workflow suitable for local and CI comparison. It may be an ignored integration benchmark, example, or dedicated tool; choose the smallest design that can control one owned or external PostgreSQL server and report structured results.

Measure at least:
- cached-image cold start through final TCP readiness;
- cold and warm template acquisition;
- sequential and bounded-concurrent create plus cleanup;
- small and representative migrated template sizes;
- explicit and deferred cleanup drain time;
- PostgreSQL admin session counts or connection attempts;
- owned-container and POSTGRES_TEST_ADMIN_URL modes;
- clean external-only compilation once that feature exists.

Record image digest, PostgreSQL version, storage driver, CPU count, template size, concurrency, and relevant harness configuration. Keep normal correctness tests deterministic and do not enforce machine-specific latency thresholds.

## Validation

Run the benchmark against current HEAD and save a human-readable baseline in project documentation or issue notes. Confirm repeated runs expose variance and that final-server readiness does not accidentally measure the temporary initdb server.

## Verified finding

README documents the core adapter contract, external admin URL, cleanup, and connection setters, but it does not provide:
- an async OnceCell or equivalent recipe that guarantees one harness and template acquisition per process;
- guidance about Cargo integration-test binaries starting separate owned containers;
- a one-service-per-CI-job external PostgreSQL example;
- a pool-sizing worksheet tied to the effective lease count;
- prewarm or deferred-cleanup guidance;
- owned-container performance-profile tradeoffs;
- an external-only dependency feature.

These omissions can cause downstream suites to repeat container startup, template coordination, authentication, or over-large application pools even if the internal optimizations land.

## Target

Publish a concise performance guide plus a tested reference adapter. Show one process-local harness and cached DatabaseTemplate, stable complete fingerprints, template use for ordinary tests, empty databases only for migration-order tests, application pools closed before lease return, and actual total pool capacity mapped to per-database permits.

Add CI examples using one PostgreSQL 18 service per job and POSTGRES_TEST_ADMIN_URL, including when cleanup_on_start(false) is appropriate for an ephemeral job. Explain that separate integration-test binaries are separate processes and how external mode enables content-addressed template reuse across them. Document owned fast storage, deferred drain, prewarming, and the external-only feature once available.

## Guardrails and validation

Examples must compile or run in documentation tests or CI. Do not recommend Testcontainers reusable-container mode, Docker CLI prefix deletion, default trust authentication, FILE_COPY without workload evidence, or transaction/schema isolation as equivalent to database-per-test safety. Include a short measurement recipe rather than universal latency promises.
